the first tank plan, in full — what the draft card would show:
  summary: A two-player split-screen tank game in the browser: each player drives a tank on their own half of the screen, shoots shells that blow holes in destructible walls, and picks up power-ups that change how their tank shoots or moves. Player one uses WASD and Space, player two uses the arrow keys and Enter; whoever knocks out the other tank wins the round.
  assumptions: 
    - Both players see the same arena from two camera views side by side, split down the middle vertically.
    - Players hide their tanks from each other by pressing a 'distraction' button that blacks out the opponent's view for a moment.
    - Tanks move in eight directions and have a rotating turret aimed with a separate pair of keys.
    - Each tank has three hit points per round; last tank standing wins the round, first to three rounds wins the match.
    - Shells bounce off the arena border once before dying, so trick shots are possible.
  1. Scaffold, config and shared state
     files: index.html, css/style.css, config/play.js, config/look.js, config/words.js, config/world.js, js/state.js, js/main.js
     Create the empty project skeleton: index.html holding only markup with links to css and scripts, the four config files with plain commented values (arena size, tank speed, shell speed, health, power-up durations, colors, all on-screen words), and js/state.js defining the game state object plus a js/main.js that runs a fixed-timestep loop and draws an empty arena. Leave input, levels and drawing o…
  2. Arena, walls and destructible tiles
     files: js/levels.js, js/world.js, config/world.js
     Make js/levels.js generate the arena from a small tile grid in config/world.js, and js/world.js draw and collide against it. Damage must be per tile: a shell hitting a wall destroys the tiles at the blast point, and tanks cannot drive through standing tiles.
  3. Tank movement and aiming
     files: js/tanks.js, js/input.js, config/play.js
     Give each tank eight-direction movement with wall and arena-edge collision, plus a turret barrel aimed by its own keys. Player one uses W A S D to move and F T G H (or Space) to aim and fire; player two uses the arrow keys and the number pad. Read all keys from js/input.js. Tanks must not shoot or move in this piece beyond driving and aiming.
  4. Shells, explosions and damage
     files: js/shells.js, config/play.js
     Fire shells from the turret tip that travel, bounce once off the arena border, destroy tiles on impact, and damage a tank that they hit. Track hit points in js/state.js and mark a tank destroyed at zero. Keep score and round wins in state; no power-ups yet.
  5. Power-ups
     files: js/powerups.js, config/play.js, config/words.js
     Spawn pickups at random open tiles on a timer: extra shell speed, a faster reload, three-shot spread, and a shield that absorbs one hit. Each lasts a fixed number of seconds from config/play.js, is announced on that player's half in the words from config/words.js, and is drawn as a distinct icon. Do not change movement or wall damage here.
  6. Split screen, rounds, HUD and sound
     files: js/render.js, js/sound.js, js/rounds.js, config/look.js, config/words.js
     Draw the arena twice into two clipped viewports with a divider, one centred on each tank, with a small colour dot pointing to the opponent when they are off screen. Add the HUD (hit points, round wins, power-up timer), round and match transitions with the win text from config/words.js, a restart key, and simple WebAudio blips for firing, exploding and picking up an item. Remove the debug full-are…
